  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n00Z upper air analysis showed northwest to nearly zonal mid-level\nflow aloft across the Lower Mississippi Valley this evening with\na southern stream upper-level low located near Baja California.\nMeanwhile, this evening's surface analysis placed a 1024 mb\nsurface high over the Middle and Lower Mississippi Valleys. 00Z\nupper air soundings from surrounding sites indicated a\nsubstantial amount of dry air present. Late evening temperatures\nranged from the upper 30s to lower 40s.\n\nMostly clear skies and light to nearly calm winds will provide a\nconducive environment for excellent radiational cooling\novernight. Temperatures are expected to fall back into the 30s\nacross most areas. Latest NBM data indicates there is a medium\n(30-50%) chance of temperatures at or below 32 degrees in a few\nfavored areas of West Tennessee by Sunday morning. Latest METAR\nobservations show temperatures approaching surface dewpoints\nacross portions of northeast Arkansas, the Missouri Bootheel, and\nWest Tennessee. NBM/REFS data indicates a low to medium (30-50%)\nchance for visibilities dropping to one half mile or less west of\nthe Mississippi River and lower probabilities east for more\nimpactful fog. Fog will likely be concentrated along area streams\neast of the Mississippi River with better coverage west. Some\nfrost may also accompany the fog Sunday morning. Conditions will\nbe monitored through the early morning hours for potential Fog\nAdvisories if conditions warrant. However, no frost or freeze\nheadlines will be considered with the end of the growing season\nback on October 31st.\n\nTemperatures are expected to moderate into early week as the\nlatest deterministic and ensemble model trends suggest the\nsouthern stream upper-level low will move into the Southern\nPlains and weaken into an open wave trough as it moves into the\nregion by mid week. This open wave is expected to be eventually\nabsorbed by a northern stream trough rotating through the eastern\nU.S by mid to late week. This will bring a return of rain chances\nto the area with amounts expected to be light at best. A return\nof much cooler air will follow this upper-level trough for late\nweek into next weekend.",
